    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21866",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274880/assets/b027faf72c9cf6e8a7cde32a71e9d4db4c761ef433520d98d232f80b7537fba0.svg)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nUse this Fourier series to find the sum of the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice that at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left((2n-1)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi\\right) = (-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ into the given Fourier series gives\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin\\left((2n-1)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n= \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)}(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ the Fourier series converges to $f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [0,\\pi),$ we have\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (2n-1)}(-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} = \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}}.\n$$",
